{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":32500875,"ns":0,"title":"Dopaminergic cell groups","revisions":[{"revid":1331125905,"parentid":1331125553,"minor":false,"timestamp":"2026-01-04T18:11:08Z","size":8659,"sha1":"c146c36c877722fdcee4ef99fe00bbb55dc1387d","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|Groups of neurons that synthesize dopamine}}\n{{Infobox anatomy\n| Name = Dopaminergic cell groups\n| Latin = cellulae dopaminergicae\n}}\n'''Dopaminergic cell groups''', '''DA cell groups''', or '''dopaminergic nuclei''' are collections of [[neuron]]s in the [[central nervous system]] that synthesize the [[neurotransmitter]] [[dopamine]].<ref name=\"braininfo\">{{cite web |title=Dopaminergic cell groups |url=http://braininfo.rprc.washington.edu/centraldirectory.aspx?ID=3138 |website=braininfo.rprc.washington.edu |access-date=8 August 2022}}</ref> In the 1960s, [[Dopaminergic pathways|dopaminergic neurons]] or ''dopamine neurons''  were first identified and named by [[Annica Dahlström]] and [[:sv:Kjell Fuxe|Kjell Fuxe]], who used [[histochemical tracer|histochemical]] [[fluorescence]].{{sfnp|Fuxe|Hoekfelt|Ungerstedt|1970}} The subsequent discovery of genes encoding enzymes that synthesize dopamine, and transporters that incorporate dopamine into synaptic vesicles or reclaim it after synaptic release, enabled scientists to identify dopaminergic neurons by labeling gene or protein expression that is specific to these neurons.\n\nIn the mammalian brain, dopaminergic neurons form a semi-continuous population extending from the midbrain through the forebrain, with eleven named collections or clusters among them.{{sfnp|Smeets|Reiner|1994}}{{sfnp|Felten|Sladek Jr|1983}}{{sfnp|Dubach|1994}}\n<!-- NB - All sections are redirect targets for article pages that previously existed as very short stubs. Please tidy up after yourself if you choose to rename any of the sections below. Thank you. -->\n\n== Cell group A8 ==\nGroup A8 is a small group of dopaminergic cells in rodents{{sfnp|Dahlstrom|Fuxe|1964}} and primates.{{sfnp|Felten|Sladek Jr|1983}} It is located in the [[midbrain reticular formation]] [[dorsolateral]] to the [[substantia nigra]] at the level of the [[red nucleus]] and [[caudally]]. In the mouse it is identified with the retrorubral field as defined by classical stains.{{sfnp|Paxinos|Franklin|2001}}\n\n== Cell group A9 ==\nGroup A9 is the most densely packed group of dopaminergic cells, and is located in the [[ventrolateral]] midbrain of rodents{{sfnp|Dahlstrom|Fuxe|1964}} and primates.{{sfnp|Felten|Sladek Jr|1983}} It is for the most part identical with the [[pars compacta]] of the substantia nigra as seen from the accumulation of neuromelanin pigment in the midbrain of healthy, adult humans.{{cn|date=February 2022}}\n\n== Cell group A10 ==\nGroup A10 is the largest group of dopaminergic cells in the [[ventral]] midbrain [[tegmentum]] of rodents{{sfnp|Dahlstrom|Fuxe|1964}} and primates.{{sfnp|Felten|Sladek Jr|1983}} The cells are located for the most part in the [[ventral tegmental area]], the linear nucleus and, in primates, the part of [[central gray]] of the midbrain located between the left and right [[Oculomotor nucleus|oculomotor nuclear]] complexes.{{cn|date=February 2022}}\n\n== Cell group A11 ==\nGroup A11 is a small group of dopaminergic cells located in the posterior [[periventricular nucleus]] and the intermediate periventricular nucleus of the [[hypothalamus]] in the macaque.{{sfnp|Felten|Sladek Jr|1983}} In the rat, small numbers of cells assigned to this group are also found in the posterior nucleus of hypothalamus, the [[Mammillary body|supramammillary]] area and the reuniens nucleus.{{sfnp|Dahlstrom|Fuxe|1964}} Dopaminergic cells in A11 may be important in the modulation of auditory processing.{{sfnp|Nevue|Felix II|Portfors|2016}}\n\n== Cell group A12 ==\nGroup A12 is a small group of cells in the [[arcuate nucleus]] of the hypothalamus in primates.{{sfnp|Felten|Sladek Jr|1983}} In the rat a few cells belonging to this group are also seen in the anteroventral portion of the [[Paraventricular nucleus of hypothalamus|paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus]].{{sfnp|Dahlstrom|Fuxe|1964}}\n\n== Cell group A13 ==\nGroup A13 is distributed in clusters that, in the primate, are ventral and medial to the [[mammillothalamic tract]] of the hypothalamus; a few extend into the reuniens nucleus of the [[thalamus]].{{sfnp|Felten|Sladek Jr|1983}} In the mouse, A13 is located ventral to the mammillothalamic tract of the thalamus in the zona incerta.{{sfnp|Paxinos|Franklin|2001}}\n\n== Cell group A14 ==\nGroup A14 consists of a few cells observed in and near the [[preoptic area|preoptic]] periventricular nucleus of the primate.{{sfnp|Felten|Sladek Jr|1983}} In the mouse, cells in the anterodorsal preoptic nucleus are assigned to this group.{{sfnp|Paxinos|Franklin|2001}}\n\n== Cell group A15 ==\nGroup A15 exists in a few species, such as sheep, and [[Immunoreactivity|immunoreactive]] for [[tyrosine hydroxylase]], a precursor of dopamine, in many other species including rodents and primates. It is located in ventral and dorsal components within the preoptic periventricular nucleus and adjacent parts of the anterior hypothalamic region. It is continuous caudally with the dopaminergic group A14.{{sfnp|Tillet|1994}}\n\n== Cell group A16 ==\nGroup A16 is located in the [[olfactory bulb]] of vertebrates, including rodents and primates.{{sfnp|Smeets|Reiner|1994}}\n\n== Cell group Aaq ==\nGroup Aaq is a sparse group of cells located in the [[Anatomical terms of location#Directional terms|rostral half]] of the central gray of the midbrain in primates. It is more prominent in the squirrel monkey (Saimiri) than the macaque.{{sfnp|Felten|Sladek Jr|1983}}\n\n== Telencephalic group ==\nThis group is a population of cells immunoreactive for dopamine and tyrosine hydroxylase that are broadly distributed in the rostral [[forebrain]], including such structures as: [[substantia innominata]], diagonal band, [[olfactory tubercle]], [[prepyriform area]], [[striatum]] (at levels rostral to the anterior [[commissure]]), [[claustrum]], and deep [[Cortex (anatomy)|cortical]] layers of all [[gyri]] of the [[frontal lobe]] rostral to the head of the caudate nucleus; the cells are also numerous in intervening [[white matter]], including the [[external capsule]], [[extreme capsule]] and frontal white matter. They are found in the rodent, the macaque and the human.{{sfnp|Dubach|1994}}\n\n==See also==\n* [[Dopaminergic pathways]]\n* [[History of catecholamine research]]\n\n== Footnotes ==\n{{Reflist}}\n\n== References ==\n* {{cite journal|vauthors=Dahlstrom A, Fuxe K |year=1964|title=Evidence for the existence of monoamine-containing neurons in the central nervous system|journal=Acta Physiologica Scandinavica|volume=62|pages=1–55|pmid=14229500}}\n* {{cite book|vauthors=Dubach MF|year=1994|chapter=11:Telencephalic dopamine cells in monkeys, humans and rats|title=Phylogeny and Development of Catecholamine Systems in the CNS of Vertebrates|veditors=Smeets WJ, Reiner A|publisher=University Press|location= Cambridge, England|oclc=29952121|isbn=978-0-5214-4251-0}}\n* {{cite journal|vauthors=Felten DL, Sladek Jr JR |year=1983|title=Monoamine distribution in primate brain V. Monoaminergic nuclei: anatomy, pathways and local organization|journal= Brain Research Bulletin|volume=10 |pages=171–284|pmid=6839182|issue=2|doi=10.1016/0361-9230(83)90045-x}}\n* {{cite book|vauthors=Fuxe K, Hoekfelt T, Ungerstedt U |title=Morphological and Functional Aspects of Central Monoamine Neurons|series=International Review of Neurobiology|volume=13|pages=93–126|doi=10.1016/S0074-7742(08)60167-1|year=1970 |isbn=978-0-12-366813-4 }}\n* {{cite journal|vauthors=Nevue AA, Felix II RA, Portfors CV|title=Dopaminergic projections of the subparafascicular thalamic nucleus to the auditory brainstem.|journal=Hearing Research|date=November 2016|volume=341|pages=202–209|doi=10.1016/j.heares.2016.09.001|pmid=27620513|pmc=5111623}}\n* {{cite book|vauthors=Paxinos G, Franklin KB |year=2001|title=The Mouse Brain in Stereotaxic Coordinates|edition=2nd |publisher=Academic Press|location=San Diego|oclc=493265554|isbn=978-0-1254-7636-2}}\n* {{cite book|ref={{sfnref|Smeets|Reiner|1994}}|vauthors=Smeets WJ, Reiner A|year=1994|chapter=20:Catecholamines in the CNS of vertebrates: current concepts of evolution and functional significance|title=Phylogeny and Development of Catecholamine Systems in the CNS of Vertebrates|veditors=Smeets WJ, Reiner A|publisher=University Press|location=Cambridge, England|oclc=29952121|isbn=978-0-5214-4251-0}}\n* {{cite book|vauthors=Tillet Y|year=1994|chapter=9: Catecholaminergic neuronal systems in the diencephalon of mammals|title=Phylogeny and Development of Catecholamine Systems in the CNS of Vertebrates|veditors=Smeets WJ, Reiner A|publisher=University Press|location=Cambridge, England|oclc=29952121|isbn=978-0-5214-4251-0}}\n\n<!--- Categories --->\n[[Category:Dopamine]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-11-01T01:37:26Z","lastrevid":1331125905,"length":8659}]}}